rust-p2w
========

A Rust reimplementation of the p2w Python-subset -> WebAssembly (WAT) compiler,
built for the AcornSTEM K-12 IDE.

This project derives from two MIT-licensed works:

1. p2w (semantic reference + WAT output conventions)
   The Python subset accepted, the host-import contract
   (env.write_char / write_i32 / write_i64), and the runnable-module shape
   (exported `_start`) are modeled on p2w.

   p2w — Copyright (c) 2024-2026 Stefane Fermigier
   https://git.sr.ht/~sfermigier/p2w
   Licensed under the MIT License (full text below).

2. ruff_python_parser (parser design reference)
   The front-end architecture — tokenizer with INDENT/DEDENT/NEWLINE and
   bracket-depth newline suppression, plus a recursive-descent + Pratt
   expression parser — follows the design of Ruff's Python parser. No source
   is copied verbatim; if any module is later lifted directly, its copyright
   header is preserved here.

   Ruff — Copyright (c) 2022 Charles Marsh
   https://github.com/astral-sh/ruff
   Licensed under the MIT License.

================================================================================
Ideas / inspiration (concepts only — NO code read or used)
================================================================================

The projects below informed *design ideas* in AcornSTEM. No source code from any
of them was read, copied, or adapted — only public descriptions (READMEs,
overviews) were consulted. The implementations here are independent
re-expressions of the underlying (uncopyrightable) concepts. Listed for honest
attribution and to mark the clean-room boundary. License noted where known, but
nothing here depends on those licenses since no code is used.

1. Binarian (farkon00) — https://github.com/farkon00/binarian — MIT
   - The explicit `drop` keyword (visible, programmer-directed memory lifetime)
     informed our idea of leaning into Python's own `del` as a teachable
     last-use / reuse hint that the compiler can act on (drop-reuse / rc==1 fast
     path) and the IDE can visualize. See MEMORY_MANAGEMENT.md.
   - The AST-visualization tooling pattern (an opt-in graph render of the parsed
     program) informed the idea of an AST view with a lifetime/reuse overlay.

2. scikit-learn — https://scikit-learn.org — BSD-3-Clause
   - The `_repr_html_` rich-output protocol (an object exposes an HTML string;
     the host renders it inline) and its **no-JS, pure-CSS collapsible diagram**
     style (expand/collapse via CSS checkbox tricks, no scripts) informed the
     idea of a rich-output channel for the IDE: a program emits HTML that the
     runner injects into the DOM, with no-JS diagrams that survive innerHTML
     injection. Concept only — sklearn is not a dependency and does not run in
     the p2w subset.

3. MasterMath (sparkinCreations) — https://github.com/sparkinCreations/MasterMath — MIT
   - The explanatory-output pattern (step-by-step solutions + common-mistake
     warnings + contextual learning tips; "a learning companion, not a shortcut")
     informed the idea of rich, self-explaining program output (`show`/`emit_html`)
     and coaching attached to the step debugger and the concept-evidence layer.
   - Local-first persistence (IndexedDB, no accounts) as a concrete browser
     backing for the activity `set_field`/`get_field` field storage. Concept only
     — reviewed from the public README, code not read.

4. Nim (ARC/ORC memory management) — https://nim-lang.org — MIT
   - The ORC design — a trial-deletion cycle collector limited to statically
     potentially-cyclic types, with O(1) candidate registration — informs our
     planned tier-5 cycle handling (docs/REUSE_PLAN.md, COMPILER_FRONTIER.md
     task 5). Reviewed from the public docs and blog only; no source read.

5. CPython — https://www.python.org — PSF License
   - The widely documented refcount-1 in-place string-append optimization
     informed `p2w_add_assign`'s unique-receiver growth strategy. The technique
     is public knowledge; our implementation is original over our own arena
     allocator (block-size headers as capacity metadata). No CPython source
     read or used. (CPython is also the black-box *behavioral oracle* for
     tools/native_run.sh — running an interpreter to diff outputs uses it as a
     tool and imposes no license obligation on this code.)

6. MicroPython — the viper code emitter (@micropython.viper) —
   https://github.com/micropython/micropython — MIT
   - viper's documented "fast typed subset" — machine-word `int`/`uint`,
     optimized integer arithmetic and **bit manipulation** (`<<` `>>` `~`
     `&` `|` `^`), and the `ptr8/16/32` buffer types — is the design *target*
     for the fast, typed intersection of Python we compile ("cover what viper
     can do fast"). Our shifts/invert/int-bitwise deliberately match viper's
     machine-word semantics: shift counts masked mod-32; wrapping integer ops.
   - Consulted the **public documentation only** — docs/reference/speed_python.rst
     (the viper spec: types, operations, machine-word behavior). The viper
     *emitter* source (py/emitnative.c, py/asm*.c) was **NOT read**. The
     implementations here are standard WASM (i32.shl / shr_s / xor) and LLVM
     bit instructions, patterned on our own existing helpers ($py_neg,
     p2w_band). Behavior/spec is uncopyrightable; no MicroPython code is used.

7. Statically-compiled Python subsets, as a group — consulted for TYPE SYSTEM
   DESIGN DECISIONS ONLY, from public documentation. Written up in
   PRIOR-ART-TYPES.md; each decision is cited at the point it informed, in
   tests/oracle/open-question/.
   - RPython (PyPy) — https://rpython.readthedocs.io — MIT
     The rule "variables should contain values of at most one type at each
     control flow point", and the representation-derived rule that None may mix
     with pointer-shaped types but not with int, float or tuple. Both informed
     how we frame the same questions. Documentation only; the annotator and
     rtyper sources were NOT read.
   - Shed Skin — https://github.com/shedskin/shedskin — GPL/BSD
     That it uses Agesen's Cartesian Product Algorithm for parametric
     polymorphism — a published algorithm (ECOOP 1995), consulted as literature.
     Shed Skin's own source was NOT read, and note its GPL status: nothing from
     it may be used beyond the fact of the citation.
   - Codon — https://docs.exaloop.io — Business Source License
     The framing that tuples remain heterogeneous while lists must be
     homogeneous, as the answer to give a student. Public docs only; Codon's
     license makes reading its source inappropriate for this project, and it
     was not read.
   - Mojo — https://mojolang.org — proprietary
     The phrasing "the variable receives a type when it's created, and the type
     never changes". Public documentation only.
   - SPy (Antonio Cuni) — https://github.com/spylang/spy
     The blue/red "redshift" framing — expressions computable at compile time
     versus those that must run — as one of the strategies for resolving
     generic code. Public write-ups and talks only; source NOT read. (See also
     the separate SPy evaluation: build-on/port was considered and declined.)
   - Cython — https://cython.org — Apache-2.0
     That the typed/untyped choice can be made per variable by the programmer,
     as the one gradual answer in the field.
   - mypy — https://mypy.readthedocs.io — MIT
     Consulted as the definition of what Python programmers now EXPECT from
     typing, which is a pedagogical constraint on us regardless of what we
     implement.

